Looking at the diff: the singular unit `case 'day':` has been removed from the switch and replaced with `case "":`. That replacement is dead code — the regex's unit group `(milliseconds?|...)?` is either skipped (yielding `undefined`, which the `||` coerces to `'ms'`) or matches one of the non-empty alternatives; it can never yield the empty string. So the effect is simply that the singular unit `day` is no longer handled: `ms('1day')`, `ms('1 day')`, `ms('1DAY')`, etc. now fall through to `default` and return `undefined` instead of `86400000`. The library's own suite only exercises the plural (`'2 days'`, `'1d'`), so it stays green while this regression slips through.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var ms = require('./');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('parses the singular unit "day"', function () {
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1day'), 86400000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('2 day'), 172800000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1DAY'), 86400000);
  });
});
"}, "note": "The change deletes `case 'day':` and swaps in `case "":`, which is unreachable because the optional regex group yields `undefined` (coerced to 'ms') when absent, never the empty string. Strings using the singular unit like '1day' or '1 day' now return undefined instead of 86400000, while the existing tests only cover 'days' and 'd'."}
```

